[
    {
        "content": "<p>I've been wondering whether the definition of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Ideal.ramificationIdx#doc\">docs#Ideal.ramificationIdx</a> could be improved. It's the correct definition in the case of Dedekind domains, but it's always felt a bit clunky to me, and you can't prove anything serious (e.g., <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Ideal.ramificationIdx_algebra_tower#doc\">docs#Ideal.ramificationIdx_algebra_tower</a>) without assuming that everything is a Dedekind domain.</p>\n<p>Here's one possibility that I've been toying with recently:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ramificationIdx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ideal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ideal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsPrime</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">letI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Sq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Localization</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">AtPrime</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">q</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">length</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Sq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Sq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⧸</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">algebraMap</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Sq</span><span class=\"o\">)))</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toNat</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>With this definition, I have a proof of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Ideal.ramificationIdx_algebra_tower#doc\">docs#Ideal.ramificationIdx_algebra_tower</a> under a mild flatness assumption:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ramificationIdx_tower</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsScalarTower</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ideal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ideal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsPrime</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ideal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsPrime</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">LiesOver</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Flat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Localization</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">AtPrime</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Localization</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">AtPrime</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"o\">)]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ramificationIdx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ramificationIdx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ramificationIdx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>And I suspect you could also prove <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Ideal.sum_ramification_inertia#doc\">docs#Ideal.sum_ramification_inertia</a> in greater generality as well, although I haven't given this much thought.</p>\n<p>Would this sort of refactor be desirable?</p>\n<p>At the very least, should we replace the <code>RingHom</code> in the current definition of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Ideal.ramificationIdx#doc\">docs#Ideal.ramificationIdx</a> with an <code>Algebra</code> assumption (as has already been done for <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Ideal.inertiaDeg#doc\">docs#Ideal.inertiaDeg</a>)?</p>",
        "id": 581465058,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1774383382
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The Algebra suggestion makes sense. Presumably your proposal to change the definition actually changes its value sometimes? Isn't the current definition quite clean somehow? What's the generality you imagine the ramification inertia result holding in? I always felt that it was true in the 1d case because R/P^nR had size |R/PR|^n in the 1d case and this is not true for higher dimensional rings. Oh but is your point that your definition gets around this because it returns an appropriate e?</p>",
        "id": 581489227,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1774394244
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, this would involve changing the value of the ramification index in some cases. I'm not done with the ramification-inertia yet, but so far I haven't used anything beyond finite + flat: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/37130\">#37130</a></p>",
        "id": 581490016,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1774394730
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(so far, the theory's been nice enough that having this definition around seems potentially useful, in which case the question would be whether it should supersede the existing definition)</p>",
        "id": 581490361,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1774394954
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Well here's the refactor to remove the ring hom: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/37156\">#37156</a>. The diff is pretty clean, but a quick review by anyone would be more than welcome, since <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"488648\">@Xavier Roblot</span> has active PRs in this part of the library that will likely conflict.</p>",
        "id": 581580080,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1774435397
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm not sure what the best definition of the ramification index would be. But as a first step, removing the ring hom seems like a good idea, even though I was always reluctant to submit a PR in that direction, as I assumed there were cases where it was useful.</p>",
        "id": 581585654,
        "sender_full_name": "Xavier Roblot",
        "timestamp": 1774436807
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I am 100% in favor of removing the ring hom. <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"253861\">@Thomas Browning</span> do you know why it was there in the first place?</p>",
        "id": 581586121,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1774436957
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Seems to be purely historical (see <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"238446\">@Anne Baanen</span>'s comment on <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/19847\">#19847</a>).</p>",
        "id": 581587064,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1774437258
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>OK, let's nuke it.</p>",
        "id": 581590898,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1774438336
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Could we/should we also get rid of the prime downstairs?</p>",
        "id": 581676496,
        "sender_full_name": "Andrew Yang",
        "timestamp": 1774458335
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Certainly possible (would require making the downstairs ring explicit). And probably fine, at least for <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Ideal.inertiaDeg#doc\">docs#Ideal.inertiaDeg</a> (which defaults to 0 whenever the downstairs prime is not what you expect).</p>",
        "id": 581677127,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1774458527
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Hmmm, currently a lot of the API for ramification index mentions the prime underneath quite explicitly, which would make a refactor a bit annoying.</p>",
        "id": 581679454,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1774459204
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I am definitely less of a fan of this option. I think we would lose a lot of flexibility.</p>",
        "id": 581680031,
        "sender_full_name": "Xavier Roblot",
        "timestamp": 1774459353
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>What flexibility would you lose? Would you ever apply this to a prime down stairs that is not equal to the preimage of the prime upstairs?</p>",
        "id": 581680339,
        "sender_full_name": "Andrew Yang",
        "timestamp": 1774459450
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Well, if you fix a prime at the top of a tower, then there are two non-defeq ways of writing the prime at the bottom of the tower, and if you start looking at the residue fields which now carry data, I could imagine you could run into some trouble.</p>",
        "id": 581681138,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1774459654
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think in many situations, we want to use this with a prime below that happens to be equal to the comap of the upstairs prime, but is not necessarily definitionally equal to it. For example, if you start with a prime in the base ring and just pick a prime above it in the top ring.</p>",
        "id": 581681276,
        "sender_full_name": "Xavier Roblot",
        "timestamp": 1774459690
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In that case, they should be accomodated in API lemmas not in the definition itself.</p>",
        "id": 581681343,
        "sender_full_name": "Andrew Yang",
        "timestamp": 1774459714
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>i.e. you should have a definition <code>ramificationIdx R P</code> and show that it is equal to <code>(p.map (algebraMap R S)).factors.count P</code> (or something similar) for any <code>p</code> under <code>P</code>.</p>",
        "id": 581681592,
        "sender_full_name": "Andrew Yang",
        "timestamp": 1774459787
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>We interrupt our regularly scheduled programming to bring you this important PR: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/37173\">#37173</a> (not really that important, just splitting off the basic API for ramification and inertia from the big file).</p>",
        "id": 581683629,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1774460404
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"439483\">Andrew Yang</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Improving.20the.20definition.20of.20ramification.20index.3F/near/581681592\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>i.e. you should have a definition <code>ramificationIdx R P</code> and show that it is equal to <code>(p.map (algebraMap R S)).factors.count P</code> (or something similar) for any <code>p</code> under <code>P</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Ah, so you're suggesting that all of the API lemmas retain both <code>p</code> and <code>P</code> with a <code>LiesOver</code> assumption?</p>",
        "id": 581683741,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1774460433
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Actually, here's another thing. Should the inertia degree be defined as the finrank of the residue fields (<a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Ideal.ResidueField#doc\">docs#Ideal.ResidueField</a>), rather than the finrank of the quotients? At least, that's looking like the right definition to make the general ramification-inertia formula hold.</p>",
        "id": 581780360,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1774485479
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The only small drawback I might see is that it forces the requirement <code>[I.IsPrime]</code> and I would wonder if there are not extreme cases where we don't want this right into the definition.</p>",
        "id": 584136459,
        "sender_full_name": "Filippo A. E. Nuccio",
        "timestamp": 1775627221
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe an unrelated sidenote: I've defined ramification index for an <code>R</code>-valued <code>AbsoluteValue</code> in our project <a href=\"https://github.com/acmepjz/lean-iwasawa/blob/master/Iwasawalib/NumberTheory/RamificationInertia/AbsoluteValue.lean\">https://github.com/acmepjz/lean-iwasawa/blob/master/Iwasawalib/NumberTheory/RamificationInertia/AbsoluteValue.lean</a> (still WIP). I'm happy to see that the definition of ramification index of ideal in mathlib will be generalized, and I can still prove that our definition of ramification index is equal to mathlib's.</p>",
        "id": 584145455,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1775631327
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"300245\">Filippo A. E. Nuccio</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Improving.20the.20definition.20of.20ramification.20index.3F/near/584136459\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The only small drawback I might see is that it forces the requirement <code>[I.IsPrime]</code> and I would wonder if there are not extreme cases where we don't want this right into the definition.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can always drop the primary assumption with a junk value. E.g., something like</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Classical</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ramificationIdx</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ideal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ideal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsPrime</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">then</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">letI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Sq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Localization</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">AtPrime</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">q</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">length</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Sq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Sq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⧸</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">algebraMap</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Sq</span><span class=\"o\">)))</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toNat</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>for which you can prove</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ramificationIdx_tower'</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsScalarTower</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ideal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ideal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ideal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">LiesOver</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Flat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ramificationIdx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ramificationIdx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ramificationIdx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Of course, this doesn't answer the question of whether there's a more mathematically meaningful definition in the case of non-prime ideals.</p>",
        "id": 584184342,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1775642007
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Quick update: The following theorem (with the new definitions of ramification index and inertia degree) is now sorry-free at <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/37130\">#37130</a>, and the commutative algebra preliminaries are slowly being PRed.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">FaithfulSMul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsDomain</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsDomain</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Finite</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Projective</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ideal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsPrime</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">finrank</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"bp\">∑</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">primesOver</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ramificationIdx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"m\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">finrank</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ResidueField</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"m\">1</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ResidueField</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 584722020,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1775841337
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This is a very beautiful theorem and (like Frobenius elements) I had no idea that something was true in this generality. I'm assuming that you can prove that this theorem specialises in the dedekind domain case to the classical theorem. Note that there does not even seem to be a separability hypothesis.</p>",
        "id": 584736396,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1775845864
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, hooking this up to the existing definitions will be next (after I finish looking into whether the hypotheses can be improved at all)</p>",
        "id": 584737810,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1775846435
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>A lot of room for improvement, it turns out:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsDomain</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Finite</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Flat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ideal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsPrime</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">finrank</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"bp\">∑</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">primesOver</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ramificationIdx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"m\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">finrank</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ResidueField</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"m\">1</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ResidueField</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 584762082,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1775857282
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm surprised -- finite flat is usually not the right assumption without noetherian hypotheses (it's usually finite projective). Still, you can't argue with a formal proof!</p>",
        "id": 584772534,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1775864507
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Over a domain, finite flat might imply projective?</p>",
        "id": 584773016,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1775865118
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Seems like it: <a href=\"https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/1943418/finitely-generated-and-flat-imply-projective\">https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/1943418/finitely-generated-and-flat-imply-projective</a></p>",
        "id": 584925233,
        "sender_full_name": "Damiano Testa",
        "timestamp": 1775892174
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>We only have <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Module.Flat.projective_of_finitePresentation#doc\">docs#Module.Flat.projective_of_finitePresentation</a> in mathlib, so perhaps Noetherian is also needed?</p>",
        "id": 584926194,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1775893168
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Isn't the point of the discussion exactly that over an integral domain it's apparently not needed? (But this is news to me)</p>",
        "id": 584926272,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1775893241
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Improving.20the.20definition.20of.20ramification.20index.3F/near/584926272\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Isn't the point of the discussion exactly that over an integral domain it's apparently not needed? (But this is news to me)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Perhaps we should add this to mathlib, too.</p>",
        "id": 584926763,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1775893922
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"439483\">Andrew Yang</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Improving.20the.20definition.20of.20ramification.20index.3F/near/581676496\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Could we/should we also get rid of the prime downstairs?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Making this change now seems rather tricky since many of the existing API lemmas for <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Ideal.ramificationIdx#doc\">docs#Ideal.ramificationIdx</a> would become nonsensical. Instead, I'm thinking of removing the downstairs prime in the new definition (<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/38062\">#38062</a>), writing better suited API, and then switching mathlib over to the new definition (this is analogous to the refactor of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Polynomial.Splits#doc\">docs#Polynomial.Splits</a> where there was an intermediate definition Polynomial.Factors to hold the new definition and better suited API).</p>",
        "id": 585715513,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1776272943
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The new proposed definitions of ramification index and inertia degree are now ready for review here: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/38062\">#38062</a> (including the removable of the prime downstairs)</p>",
        "id": 591322706,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1777367543
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>having a look!</p>",
        "id": 591328570,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1777369099
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>May I ask (mathematically) the relationships of this definition and the definition of ramification index using valuation theory, i.e. the relative index of the image of valuation? I think they coincide.</p>\n<p>The background is that it diverges with the definition using inertia subgroups for non-discrete-valuation cases (which is still often used in number theory I think), see <a class=\"message-link\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/217875-Is-there-code-for-X.3F/topic/Decomposition.20subgroup.20as.20Galois.20group.20of.20local.20fields/near/591722539\">#Is there code for X? &gt; Decomposition subgroup as Galois group of local fields @ 💬</a>. There are cases that valuation theory definition gives unramified answer, but inertia subgroups definition gives totally ramified answer.</p>",
        "id": 591725071,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1777452673
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't really understand this stuff, but I suspect it may still diverge for non-discrete valuations. My worry would be that if the valuation takes infinitely many values in an interval, would that give you an infinite chain of ideals, hence infinite length?</p>",
        "id": 591747267,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1777457609
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Quick update that the sum of ramification and inertia result is now PRed here with a few remaining dependencies: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/39189\">#39189</a></p>",
        "id": 594301188,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1778507254
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Just a quick update that the API for the new definitions is pretty much all developed now, and the first refactoring PR is ready at <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40126\">#40126</a>.</p>",
        "id": 603490574,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1781554547
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I happy to review the PR but since I practically reviewed all the dependencies I would appreciate a second opinion.</p>",
        "id": 603630177,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1781595009
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I can have a look if you want. (I am quite busy in the next few days so it'll have to wait a bit though)</p>",
        "id": 603700972,
        "sender_full_name": "Xavier Roblot",
        "timestamp": 1781609111
    }
]